They include Harvard
University's Fairbank Center,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.547
The Center for East Asian
Studies at Stanford University,

00:00:54.547 --> 00:00:57.330
The Project on China's Global Sharp Power

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:58.940
at the Hoover Institution,

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.580
The Center for Asian
Research at York University,

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.320
the Department of History
at Simon Fraser University,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.840
the Institute of Asian Research

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.560
at the University of British Columbia,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.614
The East Asian Studies
Program at Johns Hopkins,

00:01:09.614 --> 00:01:12.850
The East Asian Studies
Program at UC Santa Cruz,

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:13.840
The Global China Center

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.890
at Hong Kong University
of Science and Technology,

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.290
and the Hong Kong Institute

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.240
for the Humanities and Social Sciences

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.400
at the University of Hong Kong.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.700
So just a couple of words

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.945
about how this session will
be run before we get started,

00:01:26.945 --> 00:01:30.270
each presenter is going to
speak for about 10 to 12 minutes

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.660
and at the end of the session,

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:35.350
we will reconvene for a moderated Q and A.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:36.600
You should all know this by now,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.030
but if you have a question
at any point in the webinar,

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.280
please ask it using the Q and A button

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.180
at the bottom of your screen.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.930
In total, this webinar will last

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.730
for approximately 75 minutes.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.024
We are recording it

00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:49.611
and we will be recording

00:01:49.611 --> 00:01:53.865
only those panelists who
have agreed to be recorded.

00:01:53.865 --> 00:01:56.962
But we will send out a
link to the YouTube video

00:01:56.962 --> 00:01:59.960
in a follow-up email to all
registered participants.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:00.990
So be on the lookout for that

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.870
if you can't make it
through the entire session.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.630
- So, we designed this webinar series

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.137
with the goal of supporting
graduate students

00:02:07.137 --> 00:02:09.392
and junior colleagues in
the Chinese Studies field.

00:02:09.392 --> 00:02:11.519
Last year, one of our
webinars addressed the topic

00:02:11.519 --> 00:02:13.760
of careers outside of academia,

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.920
and we'll drop that link into the chat.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.190
And for that one,
panelists spoke about jobs

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:21.510
in government, non-profits,
and specialists books.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.520
Based on the responses
to last years series,

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.500
we decided to dedicate
one webinar this summer

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.010
to the topic of searching for jobs

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.820
outside of the United States.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:29.653
In this session,

00:02:29.653 --> 00:02:31.850
we are lucky to be joined
by four scholars of China.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.910
We'll talk about the
characteristics of the job market

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.350
where they're currently teaching,

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.520
what academic life is
like at their university,

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.130
and what challenges and opportunities

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.590
their academic systems present.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.190
We hope that this panel will be of use,

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:44.740
not only to scholars of China,

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.400
but also to candidates on the job market

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.580
in a wide variety of disciplines.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.600
To save time, we'll first introduce

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.920
all of our speakers briefly.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.877
Our second speaker is Taomo Zhou,

00:02:54.877 --> 00:02:57.110
assistant professor in
the School of Humanities

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.740
at Nanyang Technological
University in Singapore.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.680
She is the author of "Migration
in the Time of Revolution:

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.820
China, Indonesia, and The Cold War,"

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.090
out with Cornell University Press in 2019.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.030
Our third speaker, Mary Brazelton,

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.230
is university senior lecturer in

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.710
Global Studies of Science,
Technology and Medicine

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.670
in the Department of History
and Philosophy of Science

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:18.270
at the University of Cambridge.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.537
She is the author of the book,

00:03:19.537 --> 00:03:21.604
"Mass Vaccination, Citizens Bodies

00:03:21.604 --> 00:03:23.730
and State Power in Modern China,"

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.170
Published by Cornell
University Press in 2019.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:29.570
Finally, Charles Chang
is assistant professor

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.880
of Environment and Urban Studies
at Duke Kunshan University.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.640
He specializes in Computation and Design,

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.220
and his publications include articles

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.690
in the Journal of Chinese
History, Political Analysis,

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.900
and Landscape and Urban Planning.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:42.826
- Hello everyone!

00:03:42.826 --> 00:03:44.287
I'm Taomo Zhou. I'm Assistant Professor

00:03:44.287 --> 00:03:47.322
at Nanyang Technological
University in Singapore.

00:03:47.322 --> 00:03:52.190
Thank you Emily and Denise
for organizing this event.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:55.230
So I thought it's still unsafe
for many of us to travel,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:56.228
So I thought I'd give you

00:03:56.228 --> 00:03:59.990
a virtual campus tour at NTU Singapore.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.227
So, here you see the
landmark of the University,

00:04:02.227 --> 00:04:04.700
the Chinese Heritage Center.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.138
So I start with talking a little bit

00:04:07.138 --> 00:04:08.910
about my own experience.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.230
So, I graduated from Cornell in 2015.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:15.770
Went on the job market for two years.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:19.313
I was very fortunate to
have jobs offers at the end,

00:04:19.313 --> 00:04:20.674
but the whole process

00:04:20.674 --> 00:04:24.600
had been emotionally and
physically very draining.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.612
So very much echoing to what
Rovana said about relevance.

00:04:28.612 --> 00:04:30.190
As Emily mentioned,

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:33.530
my first book is about China
and Indonesia in the Cold War.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.120
So I was kind of in this,

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.900
in between place

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.241
between the feuds of modern
China and modern Southeast Asia.

00:04:40.241 --> 00:04:43.640
Although we have seen a transnational term

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:45.629
for more than a decade, most of the jobs,

00:04:45.629 --> 00:04:48.262
unfortunately in many parts of the world,

00:04:48.262 --> 00:04:51.832
are still geared towards regional,

00:04:51.832 --> 00:04:53.527
country-specific research.

00:04:53.527 --> 00:04:55.600
So somehow in Singapore,

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.830
because of it's geographical location,

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:02.440
it's economic and strategy needs,

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.650
I was encouraged to kind of cross over

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.830
between China and Southeast Asia.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:10.492
So Singapore has been my
intellectual home for six years.

00:05:10.492 --> 00:05:12.120
One year as a postdoc,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.088
and five years as assistant professor.

00:05:15.088 --> 00:05:20.088
So for those of you who are
not familiar with Singapore,

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.231
I'm just going to briefly run through

00:05:22.231 --> 00:05:24.540
the academic institutions.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.223
So there are six major universities

00:05:27.223 --> 00:05:29.965
and higher education institutions

00:05:29.965 --> 00:05:32.363
all across the island state.

00:05:32.363 --> 00:05:34.283
The National University of Singapore,

00:05:34.283 --> 00:05:36.120
many of you probably have heard about,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.080
is the flagship.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.640
Has the most longstanding,
prestigious programs

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:43.070
in humanities and social sciences.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.990
And of course, my own home institute, MTU,

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.033
will always try to challenge that.

00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:53.370
UNUS College is physically
on the NUS campus,

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:58.360
but it's autonomous, independent,
liberal arts college.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.740
And then, SMU is more
like a business school.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.893
It's located downtown, right
at the center (indistinct).

00:06:09.577 --> 00:06:11.990
And SUT I'm going to
talk about them later.

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:14.410
These are two new universities,

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:17.840
rapidly expanding their
humanities programs.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.610
So if you've never been to Singapore,

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:24.030
would you pursue an academic career here?

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:28.460
So, first thing is that the
economic academic system

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:30.780
here is modeled on the United States.

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:32.010
What does it mean?

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:35.410
It means that basically
the job application process

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:36.380
is similar, right?

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:39.572
So now it says standard
job, talk, 45 minutes.

00:06:39.572 --> 00:06:42.340
It's a interview with
the search committee,

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.450
which could be either all people

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:45.720
of your field at work.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:46.570
In my case,

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:48.545
there were historians and
also social scientists

00:06:48.545 --> 00:06:52.816
and the tenure application
process is also similar.

00:06:52.816 --> 00:06:55.210
but the major difference

00:06:55.210 --> 00:07:00.040
between Singaporean universities
and American universities

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:02.810
is that most of the institution here,

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:04.647
especially the humanities programs,

00:07:04.647 --> 00:07:05.910
are very young.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:07.670
They're kind of up and coming.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:11.800
So what does it mean for
junior scholars like us?

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.180
I think it means like
it's very bottom heavy.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.530
So when I joined my department

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.980
and there were only one
or two associate professor

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:21.230
and only one full professor,

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:23.290
so when we lost that full professor

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.574
and we're still doing a
search for a senior faculty

00:07:26.574 --> 00:07:29.510
in Asian History Broadly Defined.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:33.100
So it means that you
don't have many mentors

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:35.390
who could set your path.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:37.590
You need to forge your own paths.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.530
But the upside of that is that you have,

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:45.530
like a group of really brilliant,
dynamic young colleague.

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:49.907
And I'm really enjoying
brainstorming with my colleagues

00:07:49.907 --> 00:07:51.630
in the department.

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.790
And it also means that,

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.010
basically you even got opportunity

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.730
as a junior faculty to
create your own program.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.220
I think this is quite unique in Singapore

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:05.150
because the humanities fields
are growing very rapidly.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:06.867
So I'm going to talk a little bit

00:08:06.867 --> 00:08:10.780
about the job opportunities
available here.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:14.070
So first, you see the campus of SMU,

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:16.970
Singapore, Management University and SSUS.

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:20.090
These two institutes are
probably not that well known

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:23.870
outside of Singapore, but they
are growing very, very fast.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:28.580
They want to recruit international
talents very rapidly.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.245
So how would they do that?

00:08:30.245 --> 00:08:33.020
They will offer really attractive payment.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:36.930
So for example, later on
you'll see a job advertisement

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:40.810
for education track faculty position,

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:43.340
just keep in in mind that that
is substantially different

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:45.410
from the adjunct positions
you're gonna have

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:47.912
in the United in the sense that

00:08:47.912 --> 00:08:51.370
usually the salary is
internationally compatible,

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:54.411
it's definitely economically viable,

00:08:54.411 --> 00:08:57.890
economically stable life in Singapore.

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:02.139
So I think on that, the
Singapore system is a lot better.

00:09:02.139 --> 00:09:06.215
And also you might also see advertisement

00:09:06.215 --> 00:09:09.300
for post-doctoral fellowships at ARI,

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.000
Asia Research Institute.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.030
It's also part of the National
University of Singapore,

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:16.727
but it's kind of semi autonomous.

00:09:16.727 --> 00:09:18.287
These kinds of positions,

00:09:18.287 --> 00:09:21.520
you only start with a two
or three-year contract.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.610
However, I seen many colleagues

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.110
who kind of smoothly transition

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:29.100
from, say, a short-term
post-doctoral fellowship

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.380
to a long-term research position.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.630
So you won't be teaching,

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:35.960
but it's kind of like a
tenure track research-focused

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:39.973
position, which is also
economically viable and stable.

00:09:41.837 --> 00:09:44.790
So now I'm going to advertise
for my own university.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:48.720
So here you see our
signature building, The Hive.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.300
It's also nicknamed

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.150
the (indistinct).

00:09:53.414 --> 00:09:57.500
So NTU is located at the
far west end of Singapore.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.383
We're almost in Malaysia.

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:03.200
So some of you might see the advertisement

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.513
for this Nanyang Assistant Professorship.

00:10:06.693 --> 00:10:09.760
It's a really, really generous package

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:12.820
coming with one million
Singapore dollar research fund.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:13.930
You can still apply to it.

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:17.560
The deadline is September 30th, 2021.

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:20.770
So this position has
been geared more towards

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:24.440
engineering and natural sciences.

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:27.920
However, recently we see
a very encouraging sign

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.367
that says that my
colleague, Florence Mark,

00:10:30.367 --> 00:10:32.886
(indistinct)

00:10:32.886 --> 00:10:36.458
so we're very hopeful
that public university

00:10:36.458 --> 00:10:39.963
are going to channel more of
the funding towards humanities.

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:44.320
There's also the Presidential
Postdoc Fellowship.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:48.090
So the call is currently
closed but will reopen again

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:50.423
in January next year.

00:10:50.423 --> 00:10:51.500
It's a two year contract,

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:55.670
also coming with a very
generous salary research grant,

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:59.323
and I think there is also
relocation grant as well.

00:11:01.443 --> 00:11:03.030
So, yeah,

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:06.237
would you move here if you
never been to Singapore?

00:11:06.237 --> 00:11:08.750
How is it really like working here?

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:12.293
So I already talk about the
advantages, the upsides.

00:11:12.293 --> 00:11:16.200
Some of the issues, from
what I heard from Rovana,

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.710
it seems like Singapore
system is quite similar

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:20.230
to the Hong Kong system,

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:21.253
in a sense that this is,

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:25.690
my university in particular is very tech

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:28.140
and science heavy university, right?

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:30.080
So as scholar of humanity,

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:33.910
you are constantly asked
to prove that your research

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:35.630
is valuable, right?

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:39.350
So this kind of anxiety led
to certain inflexibility

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:41.273
in terms of your publishing outlets.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.701
Basically, my department
meticulously ranked

00:11:45.701 --> 00:11:47.080
all the journals.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:49.915
They do Tier 1, Tier 1A and Tier 1B,

00:11:49.915 --> 00:11:53.190
and I have to send screenshots

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:55.685
of my Google scholar citation report,

00:11:55.685 --> 00:11:58.460
for my annual evaluation.

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:01.000
So in other words, when I
talked to my colleagues,

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.530
I found that it's kind of similar

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:04.540
to the UK and Australia system,

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:06.840
but that's something I never heard about

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.092
when I was going through my PhD training

00:12:09.092 --> 00:12:10.910
in the United States.

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:11.890
Similar to Hong Kong,

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:14.960
there are funding opportunities around,

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:19.960
however, in terms of
how to use the grants,

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:22.630
the faculty members had last autonomy.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:25.427
So basically you have to meticulously,

00:12:25.427 --> 00:12:27.920
meticulous budget,

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:31.450
itemize at all your grants,

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:33.690
and then when you're going to use it,

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:36.440
you have to show meticulous documentation

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.430
why you're seeking reimbursement.

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:41.200
So the paperwork is a bit daunting

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:45.350
and that's, I think, is
also very energy sucking

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:48.060
and it's a big time sucker.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:50.530
So that kind of prohibit
many of my colleagues

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:53.267
from applying for research grants.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.220
But having said that,

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.230
I will still say that
Singapore is a great,

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:02.110
great place for scholars like myself

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:04.260
who study Chinese migration,

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:07.580
and also because of Singapore's location,

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:08.820
there are lots of Greece scholars

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:12.880
in Southeast Asia and South Asia.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:14.800
So that means it's also

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:18.389
a great place for those who
study intra Asia connections,

00:13:18.389 --> 00:13:21.453
China-southeast Asia
relations, migration, China,

00:13:21.453 --> 00:13:24.690
south Asia relations and migration.

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:29.370
And Rovana talk about a linguistic
environment in Hong Kong.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:32.580
Similar in Singapore. Singapore
is a multi-lingual society.

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:36.530
So Mandarin, Malay,
and Tamil are all used.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:38.410
So what does it mean for a China scholar?

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:39.980
It means that, basically,

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:43.560
you have a wider body of colleagues

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:44.720
that you can work with.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:48.430
For example, at NTU and NUS,
there are Chinese departments.

00:13:48.430 --> 00:13:50.639
It's a interdisciplinary program

00:13:50.639 --> 00:13:52.190
with scholars of literature,

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:56.170
of course, of like science
and technology studies,

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:57.240
and also of history.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.430
For example, in NTU history,

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:01.400
NTU Chinese department,

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:03.390
we have medical historian Fang Xiaping,

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:07.659
who also wrote about cholera,
someone you can work with.

00:14:07.659 --> 00:14:11.477
And also it means that
you have more students

00:14:11.477 --> 00:14:16.477
who have the capacity to work
on Chinese sources, right?

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:18.670
So it's easier in my case,

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.110
easier to recruit research assistant

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:24.100
who can help me process data in Chinese.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:27.870
And in terms of access to
data in mainland China,

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:31.630
I will say that basically,

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:32.593
I think two minutes,

00:14:32.593 --> 00:14:34.580
I'll try to wrap it up,

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:38.100
in terms of research
data in mainland China,

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:39.610
I think in the past,

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:41.530
field work is relatively easy.

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:44.010
So basically it's easy
to travel back and forth.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:47.871
And also I feel like because
the political relationship

00:14:47.871 --> 00:14:51.400
between Singapore and
China are more amicable,

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:55.150
my informants are more
relaxed, or less guarded,

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:58.020
when I tell them I'm a
scholar based in Singapore.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:01.086
After COVID, I'm still trying to carry out

00:15:01.086 --> 00:15:03.610
research through online book purchases

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:06.730
such as from (indistinct)
or collecting data

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:08.660
through my local partners.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:10.840
I think international visibility

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:12.430
is the biggest challenge

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:14.900
because in the past you
have to do long haul flights

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:18.630
for 10 AHA or AAS, but after COVID,

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:20.550
lots of the events are moving to

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:23.440
Zoom, other virtual platform.

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:25.790
I feel like there are more opportunities

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:27.900
for international collaboration.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:30.310
So that's the end of my presentation.

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:32.410
I look forward to comments and questions,

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:34.860
and I'll be happy to
talk about, for example,

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:39.360
how to balance life and work
in a place like Singapore.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:40.193
Thank you.

00:15:42.635 --> 00:15:43.653
- Okay.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:49.116
So as Denise mentioned,
my name's Mary Brazelton.

00:15:49.116 --> 00:15:52.140
I'm a senior lecturer in
the Department of History

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:56.250
and Philosophy of Science at
the University of Cambridge.

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:58.860
And if you're attending this session,

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:00.700
I'm guessing that you're

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:05.770
applying for a postdoc or possibly

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:08.150
a permanent academic position in the UK.

00:16:08.150 --> 00:16:10.660
These comments will be
focused more on the latter,

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:11.980
simply because that's my experience.

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:13.440
So what it's like to
go through the process

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:15.593
of applying for it and then
taking up a lectureship

00:16:15.593 --> 00:16:17.900
in the UK.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:19.970
And I'll start by actually
talking rather a lot

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:22.920
about logistics before I move on

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:24.300
to talking about the academic life

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:25.960
and its challenges and benefits,

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:27.910
simply because I approached
this thinking about

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:29.413
what I would have liked to know

00:16:29.413 --> 00:16:33.503
when I was at the point of
making applications like this.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:37.170
So the first thing to note

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:42.170
is what a different structure of

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:45.790
academic life exists in academic careers.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:49.230
So Taomo mentioned that
Singapore's educational system

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:50.810
is modeled on the US.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:54.090
That's not so much the case for the UK,

00:16:54.090 --> 00:16:57.190
and there's an entirely
different vocabulary of

00:16:57.190 --> 00:16:58.780
job applications as a result.

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:02.210
So a lecturer, for example,
the starting point of a job

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:03.898
is an assistant professor in the US,

00:17:03.898 --> 00:17:07.270
in the UK, it's a lecturer.

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.440
In the UK, traditionally
the title of professor

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:12.909
was a senior title that you
only received after a career,

00:17:12.909 --> 00:17:15.200
a lifetime of publishing

00:17:16.677 --> 00:17:19.010
and working in scholarship.

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:22.730
So there are useful
differences of nomenclature

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:24.040
to be aware of.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.210
There's also, I think, a
pretty fundamental difference

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:30.590
in terms of the permanent nature
of lectureships in the UK,

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:33.900
as opposed to tenure
track positions in the US.

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:38.160
In the UK, job security is more

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:41.040
disconnected from
promotion and advancement.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:44.150
So a lectureship that is permanent

00:17:44.150 --> 00:17:46.763
has good job security, generally speaking,

00:17:50.161 --> 00:17:51.540
but promotion is not guaranteed.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:53.850
You have to apply for promotion,

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:55.330
sort of going through a round,

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:56.923
a series of,

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:02.420
of applications. That
is different in nature

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:04.810
than the tenure system in the US.

00:18:04.810 --> 00:18:08.660
So I got my position in 2015,

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:11.220
and since then, I've sat on
several search committees.

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.950
So this has really informed for me,

00:18:13.950 --> 00:18:16.623
my time on both sides of
that particular table.

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:19.290
One thing to know about applications

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:21.400
is that the timeline

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:25.240
for applying for and receiving
an academic job in the UK

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:26.440
works very differently.

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:27.880
Often, there's a much shorter period

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:29.200
between jobs being announced

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:30.580
and the interview's being held.

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:33.150
It's not necessarily conforming
to the academic calendar

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:35.070
that we're familiar with.

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:37.270
Another difference that I
found really fascinating

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:39.340
was the fact that in the UK,

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:42.550
the norm is that you have all
job talks on the same day.

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:46.351
And I was actually able to
attend all the other job talks

00:18:46.351 --> 00:18:48.900
for the same position that
we were all applying for.

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:51.910
In the US, it's quite
common to be expected

00:18:51.910 --> 00:18:52.743
to go out to dinner

00:18:52.743 --> 00:18:54.680
with the people who are interviewing you,

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:57.930
in the UK, we were told as
job applicants and candidates

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:00.030
for the same job to go
and have dinner together

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:01.760
without anyone from the department along.

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:04.903
So a very different approach,
I think, to sociability

00:19:04.903 --> 00:19:09.040
in that context of job applications.

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:10.470
The job talk was also very short.

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:12.770
It was 15 minutes,

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:16.540
and it was not as important
as the formal interview.

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:19.260
So the whole process
was held over two days

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:20.930
and the formal interview on day two

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:24.163
was a much more kind of formal panel.

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:26.560
Along with that,

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:30.360
there are significant legal
restrictions on what can be

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.400
asked in those formal
interviews in front of a panel.

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:36.070
Most of the questions
in that interview were,

00:19:36.070 --> 00:19:38.470
I would say, pretty similar
to what you might get

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:43.023
in interviews for a North
American job in academia.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.600
One crucial difference
though I think is that

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:49.610
demonstrating your ability
to get external grant funding

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:50.470
is more important,

00:19:50.470 --> 00:19:53.620
and that's because of a
particular difference between

00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:57.730
North America and the UK in
terms of getting funding,

00:19:57.730 --> 00:20:02.150
both for your research and for
your postgraduate students.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:04.255
So in the UK, postgraduate students

00:20:04.255 --> 00:20:06.330
who are working towards PhDs

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:09.200
are often supported by external grants.

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:12.450
So not university funding,

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:14.980
but fellowships or projects.

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:17.560
Likewise, the research that academics do

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:20.990
is often supported by grants
that they have applied for

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:24.049
that can be several years in duration

00:20:24.049 --> 00:20:29.049
and can run into the millions
of pounds or euros in value.

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:31.790
And so often at the point of application,

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:34.190
it's really good to know that
that is part of the system,

00:20:34.190 --> 00:20:36.780
that in many cases, as
an academic in the UK,

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:41.190
you will be expected to
apply for, and receive,

00:20:41.190 --> 00:20:43.600
extensive funding from
a variety of agencies,

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:46.270
some of which I've put on the slide.

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:49.660
And this is in part because
there aren't necessarily always

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:52.240
a lot of research funds
available to universities

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:54.670
and so career advancement, in many cases,

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:57.391
is tied to your ability to think

00:20:57.391 --> 00:21:01.905
in a way that will be
conducive to getting really big

00:21:01.905 --> 00:21:05.010
scale funding and grants
that will support not

00:21:05.010 --> 00:21:09.313
just your work, but postdocs
and PhD students for years.

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:14.536
Now, if you get to the point
of negotiating a position

00:21:14.536 --> 00:21:18.900
and preparing to actually come to the UK,

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:20.340
I think it's probably worth knowing

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:22.150
that there are some key differences

00:21:22.150 --> 00:21:24.140
between North America and the UK system

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:26.360
in terms of what's possible to negotiate

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:28.250
and what's desirable

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:29.370
as a new

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:33.670
starter in a position here to negotiate.

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:37.640
So it may be quite difficult
to negotiate on base things

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:39.790
like salaries, but there are other things

00:21:39.790 --> 00:21:41.720
that are quite important to consider.

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:44.731
Notably, I would call
attention to visa fees.

00:21:44.731 --> 00:21:48.107
I think I've missed a slide
here, but that's fine.

00:21:48.107 --> 00:21:51.810
In my experience, relocation
packages are really generous,

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:54.810
but in some cases you have
to pay for your own visa,

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:57.610
which can amount to thousands
of dollars or pounds.

00:21:57.610 --> 00:21:59.470
So if you're offered a
job and you can negotiate,

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:04.240
ask whether the department
will cover those fees.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:05.073
Just to give you a sense,

00:22:05.073 --> 00:22:07.490
I ran the numbers and I found that

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:11.490
to go from receiving a job
and starting a work visa

00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:16.165
to the final point of
getting permanent residency

00:22:16.165 --> 00:22:18.041
and even citizenship in the UK,

00:22:18.041 --> 00:22:22.610
that total amount is about
8,000 to 9,000 pounds.

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:24.170
So about 10,000 US dollars.

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:26.390
So it's something that if
you can get your university

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:28.940
to pay for it, and some
universities will do this,

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:31.951
that would be extremely desirable.

00:22:31.951 --> 00:22:35.570
Also, there are other aspects of logistics

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:37.160
that I'd be happy to talk
about in the Q and A,

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:37.993
but I have a timer

00:22:37.993 --> 00:22:39.430
and I see that I'm quickly
running out of time

00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:41.153
so I'd like to transition
to talk a bit more about

00:22:41.153 --> 00:22:45.189
some of the less tangible
aspects of jobs in the UK.

00:22:45.189 --> 00:22:47.730
So first, just some
interesting differences

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:48.803
of academic life.

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:52.050
It was really fascinating
to move to the UK

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:54.840
and realize that certain concepts
and conceptual frameworks

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:56.550
could be very, very different.

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:58.847
So in the UK, empire matters hugely

00:22:58.847 --> 00:23:02.200
as a category of historical analysis.

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:03.660
And in some respect,

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:08.660
studies of non-Western regions
or histories can be assumed,

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:11.200
or even just read as studies of empire,

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:13.670
and especially of British empire.

00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:14.840
This has been interesting to navigate

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:18.150
as a historian of China,
especially as British universities

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.680
embraced projects of decolonization,

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:22.950
and that's something I'm happy
to speak about a bit more

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:23.783
if there's time,

00:23:23.783 --> 00:23:25.642
this kind of idea of
decolonizing the curriculum.

00:23:25.642 --> 00:23:27.950
But one of the points that
I do find myself making over

00:23:27.950 --> 00:23:28.840
and over in my teaching

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:30.798
is that Britain wasn't the
only the empire of the world

00:23:30.798 --> 00:23:33.596
and that empires aren't only ever Western.

00:23:33.596 --> 00:23:38.220
Anyway, that may be worth noting.

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:41.210
But given that what non-Western
history is read often

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:42.600
as Imperial history,

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:44.950
area studies can work a little
bit differently as a result

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:48.068
so often I'm surprised to find that people

00:23:48.068 --> 00:23:51.176
in the UK think of China
as part of Southeast Asia

00:23:51.176 --> 00:23:52.710
instead of East Asia.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:53.690
Coming from North America,

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:55.373
where you have those
interesting regional boundaries,

00:23:55.373 --> 00:23:58.704
that can be interesting to explore,

00:23:58.704 --> 00:24:01.060
even though of course,

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:02.250
these categories are themselves,

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:04.503
historically contingent
in all kinds of ways.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:08.760
But in general, I think
you see ideas of Asia

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:10.973
being tied much more
strongly to South Asia

00:24:10.973 --> 00:24:13.120
in really fascinating ways.

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:14.430
And again, I'm happy
to talk about that more

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:15.263
in the Q and A.

00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:18.493
Other points to note,

00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:21.830
Taoma had mentioned

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:23.180
the points about

00:24:24.770 --> 00:24:28.630
things like evaluations of
your research and teaching

00:24:28.630 --> 00:24:31.543
being tied to certain kinds
of publications and metrics.

00:24:32.678 --> 00:24:34.000
That connects to something in the UK

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:36.050
called the Research Excellence Framework,

00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:39.544
the metrication, the ways
in which your publications

00:24:39.544 --> 00:24:43.740
are evaluated by your
peers often and regularly.

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:45.547
And this happens every
five years, the REF.

00:24:45.547 --> 00:24:47.940
I'm at Cambridge and Cambridge and Oxford

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:50.343
are two universities in
the UK that I would say,

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:52.640
try to

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:56.290
protect their academics from the REF

00:24:56.290 --> 00:24:58.940
in all kinds of useful ways,

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:01.191
but it is something that's
important to know about

00:25:01.191 --> 00:25:03.350
if you are applying for a job,

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:04.750
because it is something that

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.844
you're expected to publish
a certain number of papers

00:25:08.844 --> 00:25:11.500
for each REF evaluation,

00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:13.773
and so that often
becomes part of planning.

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:16.820
So I don't wanna go on for too long.

00:25:16.820 --> 00:25:18.610
I wanna mention very
briefly some challenges

00:25:18.610 --> 00:25:20.423
and some positive aspects,

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:23.960
but I will note as well that there are,

00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:25.100
well, I'll start with the positive,

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:25.990
which is that

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:30.870
there's just briefly the
costing that I ran up.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:33.760
So coming to 9,000 pounds for a full visa

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:35.589
and permanent residency.

00:25:35.589 --> 00:25:38.880
So one of the wonderful
things about life in the UK

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:42.270
is the particular kind
of academic sociability.

00:25:42.270 --> 00:25:44.733
It's a really intense and wonderful

00:25:44.733 --> 00:25:48.870
approach to academic life, I
think in all kinds of ways.

00:25:48.870 --> 00:25:50.610
And again, I'm happy to
talk about that a bit more

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:53.210
in the Q and A.

00:25:53.210 --> 00:25:55.263
But that said, there are challenges.

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:58.920
Life in the UK, in all kinds of ways,

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:00.900
can take a lot to adjust to.

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:02.780
The weather takes a lot to adjust to,

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:04.900
the very, very short
daylight hours in the winter

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:05.950
take a lot to adjust.

00:26:07.420 --> 00:26:09.970
As an American working on
Chinese history in the UK,

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:12.100
that effectively means that life is split

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:13.160
between three continents

00:26:13.160 --> 00:26:15.870
and that is something that
others might find as well.

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:19.010
Your research and the focus of your study

00:26:21.205 --> 00:26:22.423
is in East Asia,

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:27.270
in China, your family
perhaps is in North America

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:30.420
and your job and your life are in Europe.

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:33.690
So it can be complicated by things like

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:35.533
the outbreak of a global pandemic.

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:38.040
There are cultural differences

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:39.926
that people can sometimes laugh about,

00:26:39.926 --> 00:26:42.971
the differences in terminology,

00:26:42.971 --> 00:26:45.420
but I've been really
struck by the ways in which

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:46.860
you can have a conversation with someone

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:48.760
in the context of British academia,

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:51.640
and as you can see from this
graphic that I've depicted,

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:52.790
what you think is happening

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:54.790
might actually come
across totally differently

00:26:54.790 --> 00:26:57.843
to somebody in the British system.

00:26:58.749 --> 00:27:01.700
I think I'm keenly aware

00:27:01.700 --> 00:27:04.239
that I am running out of
my allotted 12 minutes.

00:27:04.239 --> 00:27:07.540
So I just want to note that
there are particular challenges

00:27:07.540 --> 00:27:09.098
at the moment in the UK.

00:27:09.098 --> 00:27:11.180
I never thought

00:27:11.180 --> 00:27:13.890
when I came to to Britain,

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:15.654
that I would be in a position to strike

00:27:15.654 --> 00:27:20.654
and to join a union and participate
in an industrial action.

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:22.457
But in 2017

00:27:24.212 --> 00:27:27.050
and 2018, and then again last year,

00:27:27.050 --> 00:27:29.140
that is what I found myself doing as

00:27:29.140 --> 00:27:30.621
part of the Universities
and Colleges Union,

00:27:30.621 --> 00:27:35.621
particularly as a part of a
pushback across the sector

00:27:36.725 --> 00:27:40.771
against issues with pensions
for UK university staff.

00:27:40.771 --> 00:27:43.600
And so that dispute is still ongoing,

00:27:43.600 --> 00:27:45.070
but as with many other places,

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:48.225
the working conditions of
teaching staff in the UK

00:27:48.225 --> 00:27:51.220
are under constant pressure
from corporatizing interests.

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:53.320
And so that too has been a feature,

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:56.150
not just of my life as an
employee of the university,

00:27:56.150 --> 00:27:58.530
but also as a teacher, and
talking through these issues

00:27:58.530 --> 00:28:01.223
with my students in
really fascinating way.

00:28:03.603 --> 00:28:04.930
So the thing that I will end on

00:28:04.930 --> 00:28:07.886
is simply to note that one
of the really lovely benefits

00:28:07.886 --> 00:28:10.910
of my time in the UK has been exploring

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:14.240
a really rich world of sources.

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:15.890
I'm so fortunate to be at Cambridge

00:28:15.890 --> 00:28:18.531
where the Needham Research
Institute is located,

00:28:18.531 --> 00:28:21.570
with a variety of really great archives,

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:24.370
in particular for history of
science and medicine in China.

00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:26.620
But there are so many more

00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:29.260
sources that I wouldn't have
necessarily been so aware of

00:28:29.260 --> 00:28:31.313
if I hadn't been at Cambridge

00:28:31.313 --> 00:28:35.240
where there are so many valuable aspects

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:36.730
of things like the national archives,

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:38.710
the foreign office archives,

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:40.390
that shed light on Chinese history,

00:28:40.390 --> 00:28:42.960
especially in the 20th century
in really fascinating ways.

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:45.653
So I will stop there, but
thanks very much for your time.

00:29:00.339 --> 00:29:02.150
- All right.

00:29:02.150 --> 00:29:04.880
This is my turn.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:06.103
Let me share my screen.

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:20.560
All right. Hi everyone.

00:29:20.560 --> 00:29:24.847
Charles Chang, I'm to Duke
Kunshan University or DKU.

00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:29.990
I think you've learned a
lot from our other panelists

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:34.370
which showed their institutions in Asia,

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:35.543
and Europe in general.

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:39.380
And I think it will be helpful

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:42.030
if I can briefly introduce my university,

00:29:42.030 --> 00:29:43.800
which is a university in China

00:29:44.649 --> 00:29:48.343
and which might be quite
different from other universities.

00:29:49.461 --> 00:29:51.098
So our university

00:29:51.098 --> 00:29:54.683
is a liberal arts university in China,

00:29:55.550 --> 00:29:57.360
that is to say it's very different

00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:59.310
from the Chinese universities

00:29:59.310 --> 00:30:01.720
in that it's completely modeled

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:04.673
after American institutions, namely Duke.

00:30:05.690 --> 00:30:07.820
It's very small and competitive,

00:30:07.820 --> 00:30:10.600
and it's research oriented.

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:14.110
Generally, it's a joint venture university

00:30:14.110 --> 00:30:17.930
setup by Duke University

00:30:17.930 --> 00:30:18.763
and

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:20.513
Wuhan University

00:30:22.146 --> 00:30:22.979
and

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:27.160
it is located in Kunshan.

00:30:30.415 --> 00:30:32.531
(indistinct)

00:30:32.531 --> 00:30:36.114
Kunshan is like a small
country level city.

00:30:38.822 --> 00:30:41.510
It has been the richest county in China

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:43.443
for the past 15 years.

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:49.558
So Kunshan has a lot of
advanced manufacturing industry

00:30:49.558 --> 00:30:52.250
and the local government

00:30:52.250 --> 00:30:54.100
thinks that it would be a great idea

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:56.780
to promote higher education in a sense.

00:30:56.780 --> 00:30:59.673
So as a new university,

00:31:01.545 --> 00:31:06.233
we enjoyed a generous support
from the local government.

00:31:08.440 --> 00:31:10.135
What is DKU's mission?

00:31:10.135 --> 00:31:11.890
So Duke Kunshan University, like I said,

00:31:11.890 --> 00:31:14.499
is a liberal arts university.

00:31:14.499 --> 00:31:16.824
Our mission is to introduce world-class

00:31:16.824 --> 00:31:19.760
liberal arts education to China,

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:24.400
and we wanna educate classes
of international talents.

00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:27.430
So that is very different
from a traditional Chinese

00:31:27.430 --> 00:31:29.930
university that is research-based.

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:32.064
It's very similar to the
liberal arts colleges

00:31:32.064 --> 00:31:33.700
in the United States.

00:31:33.700 --> 00:31:37.587
We also wanna promote the
innovation of higher education

00:31:37.587 --> 00:31:41.040
and support high caliber research

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:42.780
and academic excellence,

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:44.653
and I will talk a little bit about that

00:31:44.653 --> 00:31:47.590
in terms of the differences from the DKU

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:51.040
under the American liberal arts colleges.

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:54.330
So in terms of size, we are quite small.

00:31:54.330 --> 00:31:59.330
We have 14 undergrad majors,
five masters program.

00:32:01.060 --> 00:32:04.100
We have current 130 faculties.

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:06.350
This is in a year of 2019.

00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:11.470
And almost at 900 undergrad students.

00:32:11.470 --> 00:32:13.190
We're still expanding.

00:32:13.190 --> 00:32:14.140
We wanna keep

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:16.360
the faculty and student ratio

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:18.740
to one to six or one to seven.

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:19.600
And then potentially,

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:22.780
we will have about 2,000
undergraduate students,

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:26.000
which means that we will
probably still recruit

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:29.783
a fairly large number of
full-time tenure track faculty.

00:32:31.640 --> 00:32:35.750
Like I said, our core mission
is liberal arts education.

00:32:35.750 --> 00:32:39.350
So we wanna educate our
undergraduate students

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:42.600
to have a proposal (indistinct)

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:48.515
globally, the (indistinct) that we have.

00:32:48.515 --> 00:32:52.993
60% of students come
from all over the world.

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:58.510
So we wanna cultivate
a international culture

00:32:58.510 --> 00:33:01.250
where a student can learn from one another

00:33:01.250 --> 00:33:05.230
and the undergrad liberal
arts education is a core,

00:33:05.230 --> 00:33:08.310
means that we, as a full-time faculty,

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:13.310
we do not get pressure to pursue
academic funding in China,

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:20.060
or publish certain journals
by some kind of metrics.

00:33:20.580 --> 00:33:23.030
In a way we are encouraged to do research,

00:33:23.030 --> 00:33:25.400
but it's not

00:33:25.400 --> 00:33:28.513
mandatory for your tenure case.

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:34.470
And in terms of the
undergraduate education,

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:37.350
we have a bit different structure.

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:39.890
We have couple of features
that are different

00:33:39.890 --> 00:33:43.490
from a lot of universities in the US,

00:33:43.490 --> 00:33:47.450
in that we have an
intensive seven week term.

00:33:47.450 --> 00:33:52.450
So in total, we have four
terms per academic year,

00:33:52.620 --> 00:33:56.010
but each term is only seven weeks long.

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:57.830
And for undergrad students,

00:33:57.830 --> 00:34:01.310
they normally just take two courses

00:34:01.310 --> 00:34:03.997
and they learn all these kinds
of things in seven weeks.

00:34:03.997 --> 00:34:07.550
And instead of the department structure,

00:34:07.550 --> 00:34:09.710
we have the division or area of knowledge.

00:34:09.710 --> 00:34:12.060
So we only have three big divisions,

00:34:12.060 --> 00:34:14.730
the Divisions of Arts and Humanities,

00:34:14.730 --> 00:34:19.730
Divisions of Social Science
and Division of Natural

00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:22.260
and Applied Science.

00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:24.377
So that is because we wanna encourage

00:34:24.377 --> 00:34:28.796
interdisciplinary studies
so that we do not want

00:34:28.796 --> 00:34:33.000
faculty in just one division.

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:36.127
So for the undergrad education,

00:34:37.324 --> 00:34:40.354
the students were asked to
take the common core courses,

00:34:40.354 --> 00:34:45.220
which includes, for example, the Chinese,

00:34:45.220 --> 00:34:48.710
a little bit of the Chinese
history and culture.

00:34:48.710 --> 00:34:49.890
And for example,

00:34:49.890 --> 00:34:54.186
the philosophical thinking, et cetera.

00:34:54.186 --> 00:34:56.880
So in that case, the student

00:34:56.880 --> 00:34:59.810
who's educated in a Chinese system

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:04.090
would know more of the
Western ideas and philosophy,

00:35:04.090 --> 00:35:06.810
as well as China's place in the world.

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:09.890
And then they also take language courses.

00:35:09.890 --> 00:35:11.010
For Chinese students,

00:35:11.010 --> 00:35:15.720
they have to take English
courses to improve their English.

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:19.330
And then they were asked
to take divisional courses

00:35:19.330 --> 00:35:22.360
and interdisciplinary courses.

00:35:22.360 --> 00:35:24.533
And also the undergrad students

00:35:24.533 --> 00:35:26.670
normally spend one year

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:33.990
abroad, in a university,
either in the US or,

00:35:33.990 --> 00:35:36.860
or other parts of the world.

00:35:36.860 --> 00:35:38.260
Most of them went to Duke,

00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:42.100
because that is an easy way for them

00:35:42.100 --> 00:35:46.727
to get these kinds of credit exchanged.

00:35:49.630 --> 00:35:51.300
And then in the fourth year,

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:53.100
they returned to DKU

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:56.704
to continue and to finish their study.

00:35:56.704 --> 00:35:57.537
Because we are still new,

00:35:57.537 --> 00:35:59.822
so we actually do not have any

00:35:59.822 --> 00:36:03.890
undergraduate students
who have graduated yet.

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:08.890
This coming year will be the
first cohort of our students.

00:36:12.320 --> 00:36:17.320
So that is the teaching and
the education part of the DKU.

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:22.320
I also wanna give you an idea

00:36:25.697 --> 00:36:26.877
about the campus.

00:36:26.877 --> 00:36:31.040
So currently we are literally
building our university.

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:33.076
We are going to build

00:36:33.076 --> 00:36:36.740
in five phases of the campus,

00:36:36.740 --> 00:36:40.570
which in total gonna be
about 200 acres of land,

00:36:40.570 --> 00:36:44.793
but currently we have
the phase one completed.

00:36:45.633 --> 00:36:48.900
So we will have couple buildings.

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:51.031
They are all designed by

00:36:51.031 --> 00:36:54.331
a company that started,

00:36:54.331 --> 00:36:56.277
and the buildings are certified by

00:36:56.277 --> 00:36:58.950
(indistinct) campus,

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:00.570
so that means we are breathing

00:37:01.790 --> 00:37:04.090
kind of like high quality air

00:37:04.090 --> 00:37:07.263
that is comparable to
Los Angeles, perhaps.

00:37:08.108 --> 00:37:10.740
We are expanding to the phase two.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:12.163
So this year,

00:37:13.900 --> 00:37:16.430
the phase two of the
campus will be complete.

00:37:16.430 --> 00:37:20.793
And in the phase two,
we will have libraries,

00:37:22.920 --> 00:37:26.100
gymnasium, and as well as
faculty and student apartments.

00:37:26.100 --> 00:37:27.083
So in a way,

00:37:28.312 --> 00:37:32.033
DKUs gonna be much nicer
in this coming year.

00:37:33.380 --> 00:37:35.490
Okay. So in the end, I
wanna talk a little bit

00:37:35.490 --> 00:37:37.580
about this hiring process

00:37:37.580 --> 00:37:40.960
which you might be very interested in.

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:43.710
So the hiring process at DKU is not very,

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:45.810
very different from the hiring process

00:37:48.334 --> 00:37:49.230
in American universities

00:37:49.230 --> 00:37:51.970
in that we have three
rounds of the interview.

00:37:51.970 --> 00:37:53.473
The first round is just like,

00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:56.650
it's conducted remotely.

00:37:56.650 --> 00:37:59.631
It's just like any universities in the US.

00:37:59.631 --> 00:38:02.570
The second round is the
interview with DKU's leadership.

00:38:02.570 --> 00:38:04.600
You will be asked to talk about

00:38:04.600 --> 00:38:09.060
your research field into this DKU's

00:38:09.060 --> 00:38:12.300
interdisciplinary
environment and how you see

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:17.050
yourself in teaching in terms
of the curriculum at DKU.

00:38:17.050 --> 00:38:18.840
The third round is quite different

00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:21.430
from what you get in the US in that

00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:26.560
we would invite the potential candidates

00:38:26.560 --> 00:38:30.062
for a campus visit
during the winter break,

00:38:30.062 --> 00:38:32.480
but because normally every year,

00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:35.743
for this year, we are hiring 20 to 30

00:38:35.743 --> 00:38:38.860
tenure track faculty
since we're expanding.

00:38:38.860 --> 00:38:41.970
So we would invite all the candidates

00:38:41.970 --> 00:38:44.287
to the campus in the same time.

00:38:44.287 --> 00:38:49.287
And this process is not to
create a competitive environment

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:52.430
between candidates, because potentially

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:54.470
if you are like a strong,

00:38:54.470 --> 00:38:56.040
you are strong candidates,

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:59.420
DKU can potentially
hire all of them, right?

00:38:59.420 --> 00:39:00.253
All of you.

00:39:00.253 --> 00:39:03.610
So in a way, this process is rather

00:39:03.610 --> 00:39:07.173
trying to observe everyone
in terms of how well

00:39:07.173 --> 00:39:11.552
you would work with
existing faculty at DKU

00:39:11.552 --> 00:39:16.552
and how you can handle,
for example, teamwork.

00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:20.420
So this on-campus visit
will take about two days

00:39:20.420 --> 00:39:22.670
where you will give a 15 minutes job talk

00:39:22.670 --> 00:39:24.240
and

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:25.193
you also will,

00:39:27.436 --> 00:39:29.560
will form a team

00:39:29.560 --> 00:39:30.610
to create

00:39:32.280 --> 00:39:35.170
sort of a imaginative major

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:37.330
and explain why you wanna do that.

00:39:37.330 --> 00:39:40.002
That is a bit different
from the interview process

00:39:40.002 --> 00:39:41.940
in the United States.

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:44.810
And once you get hired, DKU offers

00:39:44.810 --> 00:39:46.630
competitive salary

00:39:48.770 --> 00:39:51.330
and the startup fund

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:53.380
and the generous benefits

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:56.400
comparable to the US institutions.

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:57.990
The typical teaching load

00:39:59.370 --> 00:40:00.203
is,

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:07.970
the typical teaching load is
five courses per academic year.

00:40:08.380 --> 00:40:10.530
However, currently we are

00:40:10.530 --> 00:40:12.390
under four teaching,

00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:14.750
four courses per academic year

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:17.140
and the teaching's a bit flexible.

00:40:17.140 --> 00:40:18.730
So in that case,

00:40:18.730 --> 00:40:21.547
you could arrange on your teaching

00:40:21.547 --> 00:40:26.547
into two semesters that
are most convenient for you

00:40:26.740 --> 00:40:28.200
and spend some time

00:40:30.100 --> 00:40:31.633
for your own research.

00:40:38.410 --> 00:40:39.253
And lastly,

00:40:41.130 --> 00:40:42.261
okay.

00:40:42.261 --> 00:40:43.094
So lastly,

00:40:43.094 --> 00:40:44.560
I wanna see that

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:48.650
in terms of applying to the jobs at DKU,

00:40:48.650 --> 00:40:49.483
sometimes

00:40:51.970 --> 00:40:53.230
people don't realize that

00:40:56.364 --> 00:40:59.370
that unlike the application in the US

00:40:59.370 --> 00:41:03.130
where you have to be very
clear fate to a position,

00:41:03.130 --> 00:41:06.640
sometimes, because DKU's expanding

00:41:06.640 --> 00:41:11.530
so DKU's waiting to be
a little bit flexible

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:14.460
in terms of the job applications.

00:41:14.460 --> 00:41:16.200
So if you're a strong candidate

00:41:16.200 --> 00:41:19.880
and you see a position
that is related to you,

00:41:19.880 --> 00:41:23.710
however, may not be extremely related,

00:41:23.710 --> 00:41:25.280
you still should apply

00:41:25.280 --> 00:41:26.360
because in a way,

00:41:26.360 --> 00:41:30.250
maybe DKU could fit you
into another position

00:41:31.812 --> 00:41:34.980
that they are trying to expand to.

00:41:34.980 --> 00:41:38.290
So, in a way, there's
a possibility that if

00:41:39.300 --> 00:41:41.260
the position that you're applying

00:41:41.260 --> 00:41:44.383
is not completely a clear fate to you.

00:41:46.470 --> 00:41:50.123
Last, I wanna mention that I
really enjoy teaching at DKU,

00:41:51.020 --> 00:41:55.340
strangely, because here the
students are highly motivated

00:41:55.340 --> 00:41:56.773
and they are eager to learn.

00:41:58.240 --> 00:42:01.460
Even in a field of
humanities, people are very,

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:03.970
students are very interested.

00:42:03.970 --> 00:42:06.640
Also, the staff is highly motivated.

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:10.190
I've never received email reply

00:42:10.190 --> 00:42:13.351
more than 24 hours at DKU.

00:42:13.351 --> 00:42:16.863
All the staff try to be very helpful.

00:42:17.840 --> 00:42:22.840
And in the end, also, my
colleagues are very young as well,

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:26.060
and I very much enjoy the environment

00:42:26.060 --> 00:42:29.270
where everybody's trying to
build their academic career.

00:42:29.270 --> 00:42:32.450
Of course, we have many problems,

00:42:32.450 --> 00:42:36.620
especially during this COVID
pandemic as a young university.

00:42:36.620 --> 00:42:39.770
However, I think the academic environment

00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:41.740
is very encouraging,

00:42:41.740 --> 00:42:44.750
and at least I enjoy this young

00:42:44.750 --> 00:42:47.150
and the invigorating environment.

00:42:47.150 --> 00:42:47.983
Thank you.

00:42:49.270 --> 00:42:51.120
- Thank you so much to
all of our panelists.

00:42:51.120 --> 00:42:53.500
I deeply enjoyed all of your,

00:42:53.500 --> 00:42:54.950
all of your presentations

00:42:56.260 --> 00:42:58.321
and recruiting style as well.

00:42:58.321 --> 00:43:02.980
We have some questions in
the Q and A and in the chat.

00:43:02.980 --> 00:43:05.680
If you did ask a question in the chat,

00:43:05.680 --> 00:43:07.350
please go ahead and move it to Q and A

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:08.723
since we'll focus on those.

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:10.850
We'll start with a few questions

00:43:10.850 --> 00:43:12.410
that address all the panelists,

00:43:12.410 --> 00:43:15.640
and then we'll go to questions that focus

00:43:15.640 --> 00:43:18.660
on particular individuals.

00:43:18.660 --> 00:43:21.520
So the first question from our audience

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:24.187
is a two-part question for everyone.

00:43:24.187 --> 00:43:26.870
Why did you apply for
jobs outside of the US

00:43:26.870 --> 00:43:29.690
and does your future plan
involve returning to the US

00:43:29.690 --> 00:43:31.113
in an academic capacity?

00:43:32.300 --> 00:43:34.135
- Well, I can get started.

00:43:34.135 --> 00:43:37.140
When I graduated, I applied everywhere.

00:43:37.140 --> 00:43:38.720
So I landed in Singapore,

00:43:38.720 --> 00:43:42.010
largely because that was
the only offer I received.

00:43:42.010 --> 00:43:44.740
And I think one of the reasons,

00:43:44.740 --> 00:43:45.827
I didn't get the talk about it,

00:43:45.827 --> 00:43:49.130
but I think Singapore, Asia, in general

00:43:49.130 --> 00:43:52.060
is a good environment for working mothers,

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:54.780
especially academics with small children

00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:57.650
in the sense that
childcare is less expensive

00:43:57.650 --> 00:44:01.540
and it's affordable to
hire helpers or nannies.

00:44:01.540 --> 00:44:03.120
So I do find that

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:05.480
a big attraction for me.

00:44:05.480 --> 00:44:07.860
Another thing I think
is that close to family

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:10.330
because I was born and raised in China.

00:44:10.330 --> 00:44:11.590
My parents, are now in Shenzhen,

00:44:11.590 --> 00:44:13.453
it's only four hours flight away.

00:44:14.593 --> 00:44:16.670
So for me, I think

00:44:17.896 --> 00:44:19.400
it's whether going,

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:20.760
my friends always ask me

00:44:20.760 --> 00:44:22.983
are you planning to go back to the US.

00:44:23.970 --> 00:44:25.850
In my case, probably for family reasons,

00:44:25.850 --> 00:44:28.723
not in the foreseeable future.

00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:32.517
I'd be happy to hear
what the other colleague.

00:44:37.420 --> 00:44:39.640
- Yeah, I can go next.

00:44:39.640 --> 00:44:43.200
So when I apply my apply positions,

00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:46.310
I also applied everywhere.

00:44:46.310 --> 00:44:50.740
I got 16 job interviews
in the United States

00:44:50.740 --> 00:44:53.140
for tenure track positions.

00:44:53.140 --> 00:44:56.873
I have two job offers in
the end, but I choose DKU.

00:44:58.430 --> 00:45:01.130
I think I got some difficulties in the US

00:45:01.130 --> 00:45:04.690
because my background is
a bit interdisciplinary

00:45:04.690 --> 00:45:06.750
and normally in the US,

00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:08.820
the difficulty is that if
you are interdisciplinary,

00:45:08.820 --> 00:45:12.098
you have to try really hard to fit into

00:45:12.098 --> 00:45:14.490
a department environment,

00:45:14.490 --> 00:45:17.110
which it's very difficult for me.

00:45:17.110 --> 00:45:18.580
So in the end,

00:45:18.580 --> 00:45:21.023
I feel that a lot of positions in the US

00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:24.920
were not a good fit for me.

00:45:24.920 --> 00:45:28.657
And also, I spend a lot of,

00:45:28.657 --> 00:45:33.640
pretty much most of my academic
career in United States.

00:45:33.640 --> 00:45:34.473
And

00:45:35.890 --> 00:45:38.580
last year when I applied jobs at DKU,

00:45:38.580 --> 00:45:41.710
I feel like because I studied China

00:45:41.710 --> 00:45:43.700
and I spend too much time in the US,

00:45:43.700 --> 00:45:46.110
I feel my research is a bit out of parts

00:45:47.061 --> 00:45:50.660
in a way that I do need to
do some kind of field work

00:45:50.660 --> 00:45:53.490
to get to know the
situation on the ground.

00:45:53.490 --> 00:45:57.793
So I decided to move back to China.

00:46:00.220 --> 00:46:03.130
I have families back in a
Wisconsin, United States.

00:46:03.130 --> 00:46:04.730
So I do visit my family.

00:46:04.730 --> 00:46:07.140
As to the future, I don't know.

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:11.040
I think it all depends on my research.

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:14.860
If I think it's a good idea to move,

00:46:14.860 --> 00:46:16.853
then I will.

00:46:16.853 --> 00:46:20.070
But so far I've been
having a good time in China

00:46:20.070 --> 00:46:23.083
and I'm happy not to move.

00:46:27.460 --> 00:46:29.850
- So maybe I can go next quickly

00:46:30.880 --> 00:46:34.600
and just say why I decided to apply.

00:46:34.600 --> 00:46:36.600
Like everyone else, I applied everywhere

00:46:38.600 --> 00:46:40.060
and I was really, really lucky

00:46:40.060 --> 00:46:41.910
in that basically my dream job

00:46:41.910 --> 00:46:46.140
in the best department that
I could hope for came up

00:46:46.140 --> 00:46:47.230
the year that I was applying.

00:46:47.230 --> 00:46:49.163
And I got really, really lucky.

00:46:50.240 --> 00:46:51.970
And I am someone who trained

00:46:51.970 --> 00:46:53.500
in a history of science program,

00:46:53.500 --> 00:46:55.560
and so for me to be able to get a job

00:46:55.560 --> 00:46:59.333
in a department of history
and philosophy of science,

00:46:59.333 --> 00:47:02.750
the chances of that were vanishingly rare.

00:47:02.750 --> 00:47:05.440
So for me to be able to continue to work

00:47:05.440 --> 00:47:07.120
in an academic community

00:47:07.120 --> 00:47:08.790
that was organized around

00:47:09.990 --> 00:47:12.070
that field of the history of science,

00:47:12.070 --> 00:47:13.913
was really, really precious.

00:47:14.854 --> 00:47:17.498
And so for me, that was absolutely why

00:47:17.498 --> 00:47:21.453
it was barely a decision
to have to make, to apply.

00:47:22.599 --> 00:47:23.930
And then, yeah.

00:47:23.930 --> 00:47:24.763
So that's really,

00:47:24.763 --> 00:47:25.723
my answer is that,

00:47:26.713 --> 00:47:28.791
that was what I would have,

00:47:28.791 --> 00:47:31.820
the best I could have
really hoped for, I think.

00:47:31.820 --> 00:47:32.653
Yeah.

00:47:33.550 --> 00:47:34.900
- Thank you all for those responses.

00:47:34.900 --> 00:47:37.817
We have a question from
Kayhan Nahaad who says,

00:47:37.817 --> 00:47:39.620
"Thank you for this
very informative panel.

00:47:39.620 --> 00:47:41.390
How can scholars studying topics

00:47:41.390 --> 00:47:43.854
outside the bounds of East Asian
and Southeast Asian studies

00:47:43.854 --> 00:47:47.520
position themselves
competitively for employment

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:49.720
in these regions?"

00:47:49.720 --> 00:47:52.943
Or maybe how about for
employment at your institution?

00:47:54.972 --> 00:47:57.730
- Okay, I can get started since
he mentioned Southeast Asia.

00:47:57.730 --> 00:47:59.280
So for Singapore,

00:47:59.280 --> 00:48:02.150
Singapore has been a
very cosmopolitan place,

00:48:02.150 --> 00:48:04.220
but I think after pandemic,

00:48:04.220 --> 00:48:06.130
we see similar trends all around the world

00:48:06.130 --> 00:48:07.880
is becoming more insular.

00:48:07.880 --> 00:48:12.310
So that tendency also affect
how you position yourself

00:48:12.310 --> 00:48:14.470
when applying for a job in Singapore.

00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:16.465
So for Southeast Asianers,

00:48:16.465 --> 00:48:19.040
I think it's generally,

00:48:19.040 --> 00:48:22.260
Singapore is generally very
friendly to Southeast Asianers

00:48:22.260 --> 00:48:23.770
because Southeast Asia

00:48:23.770 --> 00:48:26.400
is strategically important to Singapore,

00:48:26.400 --> 00:48:29.370
How you can tailor your project,

00:48:29.370 --> 00:48:32.400
for example, if you can
effectively tailor your project

00:48:32.400 --> 00:48:36.530
to some of the governmental
agendas in Singapore,

00:48:36.530 --> 00:48:39.300
meaning, for example, smart cities,

00:48:39.300 --> 00:48:43.000
urban development, science and technology,

00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:46.340
intra Asia regional connectivity,

00:48:46.340 --> 00:48:48.180
I think these are the key words.

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:49.440
So for example,

00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:52.310
I mentioned earlier my
colleague, Florence Mark

00:48:52.310 --> 00:48:54.690
who recently got his Nanyang
assistant professorship,

00:48:54.690 --> 00:48:57.580
I think she's very savvy
in framing her project,

00:48:57.580 --> 00:49:01.452
not just as about Hong Kong,
it's about decolonization,

00:49:01.452 --> 00:49:03.630
and it's also about urban development

00:49:03.630 --> 00:49:05.920
in the global cities of Asia.

00:49:05.920 --> 00:49:09.640
So definitely, cager it more towards

00:49:09.640 --> 00:49:12.100
some of the official agenda in Singapore

00:49:12.100 --> 00:49:12.933
is very important.

00:49:12.933 --> 00:49:14.700
Not just for a job classification,

00:49:14.700 --> 00:49:16.863
but also for grant application as well.

00:49:20.820 --> 00:49:21.943
- I can go next.

00:49:24.767 --> 00:49:25.908
So to DKU,

00:49:25.908 --> 00:49:29.896
I don't think studying
something outside of Asia

00:49:29.896 --> 00:49:34.896
is a problem because we
sometimes intentionally hire

00:49:35.110 --> 00:49:38.630
faculty who teaches

00:49:40.589 --> 00:49:42.390
on areas outside of Asia.

00:49:42.390 --> 00:49:47.390
For example, we have faculty
who teach American politics,

00:49:47.630 --> 00:49:49.320
and that is appealing part

00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:54.320
because we try to promote
international higher education

00:49:55.480 --> 00:49:59.549
and our students are very
interested in knowledge.

00:49:59.549 --> 00:50:01.680
Something like even

00:50:04.210 --> 00:50:05.480
Judaism or even

00:50:06.663 --> 00:50:09.650
European history.

00:50:09.650 --> 00:50:12.860
So in a way, it's not a disadvantage

00:50:12.860 --> 00:50:14.150
if you study something

00:50:15.080 --> 00:50:17.493
that is not based in Asia.

00:50:20.870 --> 00:50:23.166
- Maybe I can follow the same order

00:50:23.166 --> 00:50:25.310
as the previous question.

00:50:25.310 --> 00:50:27.970
So just for the part of
the UK, to be honest,

00:50:27.970 --> 00:50:30.690
I think my advice on this question

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:34.670
would be to just pay very close attention

00:50:34.670 --> 00:50:36.200
to the department or faculty

00:50:36.200 --> 00:50:37.460
that is advertising the job,

00:50:37.460 --> 00:50:39.700
which I realize is quite general advice,

00:50:39.700 --> 00:50:42.550
but I think it's particularly applicable

00:50:42.550 --> 00:50:45.630
in a case like Cambridge
or other UK universities

00:50:45.630 --> 00:50:49.070
where the institutional
configurations can be very unique.

00:50:49.070 --> 00:50:50.863
So for example, at Cambridge,

00:50:52.210 --> 00:50:55.030
the history faculty, up until recently,

00:50:55.030 --> 00:50:58.010
had one person working on Southeast Asia

00:50:58.010 --> 00:51:00.860
who connected to various aspects

00:51:00.860 --> 00:51:03.683
of overseas Chinese communities.

00:51:04.880 --> 00:51:06.815
Rachel Leo who's worked,

00:51:06.815 --> 00:51:08.610
I'm sure I'm underselling
in all kinds of ways,

00:51:08.610 --> 00:51:11.217
but most of the teaching

00:51:11.217 --> 00:51:13.260
and what we would consider Asian history

00:51:13.260 --> 00:51:14.470
was happening in the faculty

00:51:14.470 --> 00:51:16.380
of Asian and middle Eastern studies.

00:51:16.380 --> 00:51:17.640
So a totally different faculty

00:51:17.640 --> 00:51:20.500
and I think possibly
even a separate school

00:51:20.500 --> 00:51:21.763
within the university.

00:51:23.054 --> 00:51:24.310
And there wasn't any,

00:51:24.310 --> 00:51:27.863
my position was created de novo, new,

00:51:28.820 --> 00:51:30.380
in history and philosophy of science.

00:51:30.380 --> 00:51:31.213
So,

00:51:32.070 --> 00:51:33.960
really, I think it's
probably just a lesson

00:51:33.960 --> 00:51:36.180
to think carefully about

00:51:36.180 --> 00:51:39.020
the department that's
advertising the position

00:51:39.020 --> 00:51:43.940
and how your work would fit
into the existing faculty's

00:51:43.940 --> 00:51:47.880
research interests and themes
really as much as anything.

00:51:47.880 --> 00:51:50.560
- Thank you. We actually
have two questions

00:51:50.560 --> 00:51:53.380
about where you find job
announcements outside of the US

00:51:53.380 --> 00:51:54.550
so I wonder if we could go around

00:51:54.550 --> 00:51:58.060
and just in a quick
list, name the top sites

00:51:58.060 --> 00:52:01.580
where you found postings
about either your positions

00:52:01.580 --> 00:52:03.193
or international positions.

00:52:12.250 --> 00:52:14.837
- So I think we all check on the H-Net.

00:52:15.868 --> 00:52:18.128
I've been away from the job market a while

00:52:18.128 --> 00:52:20.980
so I'm not familiar with H-Net,

00:52:20.980 --> 00:52:24.133
but I agree, some of the
universities outside the US

00:52:24.133 --> 00:52:27.430
are not very consistent
with posting all their jobs

00:52:27.430 --> 00:52:28.840
on this platform,

00:52:28.840 --> 00:52:31.600
so I'd be happy to send the
links to the two positions

00:52:31.600 --> 00:52:34.650
I talk about at my home institution.

00:52:34.650 --> 00:52:36.580
And another thing I
recently thought very useful

00:52:36.580 --> 00:52:38.610
is academic Twitter.

00:52:38.610 --> 00:52:40.240
So lots of job posts.

00:52:40.240 --> 00:52:44.383
I got notified on Twitter in firsthand.

00:52:49.180 --> 00:52:50.641
- Yes,

00:52:50.641 --> 00:52:52.960
I did the same thing.

00:52:52.960 --> 00:52:56.100
I look at jobs at H-Net

00:52:56.100 --> 00:53:01.030
and I also joined a lot of mailing lists,

00:53:01.030 --> 00:53:04.388
as well as looking
through this job posting

00:53:04.388 --> 00:53:08.633
different kind of associations website.

00:53:09.570 --> 00:53:13.130
Also, I just use Google a lot.

00:53:13.130 --> 00:53:15.301
Actually, there is a lot of jobs

00:53:15.301 --> 00:53:18.180
if you just search your field

00:53:19.256 --> 00:53:21.730
and is in tenure track on Google,

00:53:21.730 --> 00:53:24.450
and that would pop up
a lot of information.

00:53:24.450 --> 00:53:26.573
So I look at that as well.

00:53:29.600 --> 00:53:32.970
- So there's one website
for UK academic jobs

00:53:32.970 --> 00:53:35.950
where I think all of them are posted,

00:53:35.950 --> 00:53:37.973
which is jobs.ac.uk,

00:53:39.299 --> 00:53:41.920
and that gives you a
smorgasbord of different options

00:53:41.920 --> 00:53:44.870
and you can filter it as needed,

00:53:44.870 --> 00:53:48.120
but that's what I would
normally direct people to

00:53:48.120 --> 00:53:50.003
for looking at UK positions.

00:53:51.370 --> 00:53:52.720
- Thank you so much for this.

00:53:52.720 --> 00:53:54.860
I see that we're almost out of time.

00:53:54.860 --> 00:53:58.070
I was thinking that perhaps
we could ask one teaching

00:53:58.070 --> 00:53:59.658
and one research question that

00:53:59.658 --> 00:54:02.720
any one of you can respond to if you like,

00:54:02.720 --> 00:54:04.840
it doesn't have to be everyone.

00:54:04.840 --> 00:54:07.079
But we have a question from Joshua Tan.

00:54:07.079 --> 00:54:09.300
What are some of the
challenges and opportunities

00:54:09.300 --> 00:54:13.080
regarding teaching or course
design for non-US contexts?

00:54:13.080 --> 00:54:14.910
In other words,

00:54:14.910 --> 00:54:15.743
are

00:54:17.361 --> 00:54:20.590
the syllabi that you've
designed in the US,

00:54:20.590 --> 00:54:22.913
are they relevant and
how did you adapt yours?

00:54:24.610 --> 00:54:28.960
- I think being Asia allow
you to teach more niche topics

00:54:28.960 --> 00:54:30.680
because the students have,

00:54:30.680 --> 00:54:33.290
they really have a good
background knowledge

00:54:33.290 --> 00:54:34.990
about the region

00:54:34.990 --> 00:54:36.920
in the pre-university level education.

00:54:36.920 --> 00:54:40.830
For example, I teach topics
such as Modern Indonesia,

00:54:40.830 --> 00:54:42.120
World War II in Southeast Asia.

00:54:42.120 --> 00:54:45.430
This topic probably are
not really pragmatic

00:54:45.430 --> 00:54:47.520
in the Western context,

00:54:47.520 --> 00:54:50.043
but in terms of the general curriculum

00:54:50.043 --> 00:54:52.220
such as introduction to Asia Pacific,

00:54:52.220 --> 00:54:55.290
I found it really interesting to re-frame

00:54:55.290 --> 00:54:58.750
some of the debates and topics in the Asia

00:54:58.750 --> 00:55:00.289
context, in the sense that

00:55:00.289 --> 00:55:04.510
what we teach, what we
research are usually framed by

00:55:04.510 --> 00:55:06.540
the political discourse,

00:55:06.540 --> 00:55:09.800
or the larger political
context where we're situated.

00:55:09.800 --> 00:55:11.720
So it's kind of fun

00:55:11.720 --> 00:55:14.290
to rework all these frameworks

00:55:14.290 --> 00:55:17.260
and reshape the, for
example, discussion question

00:55:17.260 --> 00:55:18.570
or debate topics.

00:55:18.570 --> 00:55:20.250
But I think the general,

00:55:20.250 --> 00:55:21.210
in the question,

00:55:21.210 --> 00:55:25.860
what you read or work on
during your qualifications,

00:55:25.860 --> 00:55:28.130
that's still very useful,

00:55:28.130 --> 00:55:31.193
but you probably need to
frame it in a different way.

00:55:35.570 --> 00:55:39.260
- Thank you. Does anybody else
wanna chime in on teaching?

00:55:39.260 --> 00:55:40.970
- I was just gonna briefly say that

00:55:40.970 --> 00:55:43.450
teaching at Cambridge, and more generally,

00:55:43.450 --> 00:55:46.190
in the Oxford system
is so weird and unusual

00:55:46.190 --> 00:55:48.292
that I had to learn a whole new vocabulary

00:55:48.292 --> 00:55:50.920
where students aren't in years,

00:55:50.920 --> 00:55:52.873
they're in part two or part three.

00:55:53.800 --> 00:55:54.980
And I think for me,

00:55:54.980 --> 00:55:57.780
the biggest change was
giving many fewer lectures

00:55:59.124 --> 00:56:00.270
and therefore having to squeeze

00:56:00.270 --> 00:56:02.570
and compress a lot more
material very quickly,

00:56:02.570 --> 00:56:05.090
especially because the very
specialized nature of the UK

00:56:05.090 --> 00:56:07.490
educational system means that

00:56:07.490 --> 00:56:11.590
students only study a few topics
before going to university.

00:56:11.590 --> 00:56:14.080
And so they have a lot of
really intense knowledge

00:56:14.080 --> 00:56:15.490
about several topics,

00:56:15.490 --> 00:56:17.530
but not necessarily a
breadth of knowledge,

00:56:17.530 --> 00:56:20.750
not necessarily very much
knowledge at all about

00:56:20.750 --> 00:56:22.500
Asia or Asian history in particular.

00:56:22.500 --> 00:56:24.660
So that can be a challenge,

00:56:24.660 --> 00:56:27.660
but also really fascinating and
rewarding in different ways.

00:56:29.318 --> 00:56:30.151
- Thank you so much.

00:56:30.151 --> 00:56:31.200
- [Denise] Thank you so much.

00:56:31.200 --> 00:56:32.143
- I think for me,

00:56:33.912 --> 00:56:36.350
it's because China and
the US are so different,

00:56:36.350 --> 00:56:40.400
so sometimes my students
do not know certain things

00:56:40.400 --> 00:56:44.120
that have been censored
by the Chinese government.

00:56:44.120 --> 00:56:47.352
So in my class I would
actually teach them.

00:56:47.352 --> 00:56:51.130
I would mention things
like Tiananmen Square event

00:56:52.530 --> 00:56:53.770
among other things.

00:56:53.770 --> 00:56:55.600
And I would encourage my students

00:56:55.600 --> 00:56:58.310
to use Google and stuff instead of Baidu

00:56:58.310 --> 00:57:00.500
if you can imagine that they're in China.

00:57:00.500 --> 00:57:01.570
Other than that,

00:57:01.570 --> 00:57:04.310
actually, I find that students
are really interesting

00:57:04.310 --> 00:57:06.330
once they started to
learn this kind of stuff,

00:57:06.330 --> 00:57:09.260
they feel very, I guess inspired

00:57:09.260 --> 00:57:10.800
and that they tend to ask me a lot

00:57:10.800 --> 00:57:12.030
of questions about

00:57:12.970 --> 00:57:14.640
things that were taught

00:57:14.640 --> 00:57:17.564
either in Chinese history

00:57:17.564 --> 00:57:22.357
or in a Western philosophy
or something like that.

00:57:22.357 --> 00:57:23.390
And that part

00:57:24.623 --> 00:57:27.493
is a great rewarding experience for me.

00:57:29.472 --> 00:57:31.360
- Thank you so much for
sharing those experiences.

00:57:31.360 --> 00:57:33.530
Let's ask a final research question

00:57:33.530 --> 00:57:37.200
that I will tag on a couple
of the individual questions.

00:57:37.200 --> 00:57:41.190
So an anonymous attendee
asks about human subjects,

00:57:41.190 --> 00:57:44.230
research infrastructure
protocol in your institution.

00:57:44.230 --> 00:57:46.828
For example, if you use
ethnographic methods.

00:57:46.828 --> 00:57:50.561
So that's a question
directly for the whole panel.

00:57:50.561 --> 00:57:55.561
- I think doing research in
China's great because in a way

00:57:55.790 --> 00:57:58.019
you can do your field work all the time

00:57:58.019 --> 00:57:59.980
by just talking to people

00:57:59.980 --> 00:58:01.893
and trying different kinds of things.

00:58:03.190 --> 00:58:06.070
The recent crack down on academia

00:58:06.070 --> 00:58:08.960
does not really affect us much.

00:58:08.960 --> 00:58:12.160
Frankly, when we designed DKU,

00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:13.980
I learned that

00:58:13.980 --> 00:58:18.980
we pretty much wanna protect
academic freedom at DKU.

00:58:19.310 --> 00:58:22.850
Meaning that you teach
whatever you like to teach

00:58:22.850 --> 00:58:26.400
and in the end, DKU will defend you

00:58:26.400 --> 00:58:31.400
if that is thing is problematic
by the Chinese government.

00:58:31.430 --> 00:58:35.483
But of course, we are
speaking outside of DKU.

00:58:36.541 --> 00:58:38.800
You will have to take a personal risk

00:58:38.800 --> 00:58:41.623
in terms of what you say and what you do,

00:58:44.146 --> 00:58:46.200
but my experience is that

00:58:46.200 --> 00:58:48.180
I do research on religion.

00:58:48.180 --> 00:58:50.940
For example, early this year,

00:58:50.940 --> 00:58:53.467
I visited (indistinct)

00:58:53.467 --> 00:58:56.097
et cetera, et cetera,

00:58:56.097 --> 00:58:59.320
and I didn't find it problematic at all.

00:58:59.320 --> 00:59:01.760
So in a way, I mean,

00:59:01.760 --> 00:59:04.270
if you learn those kinds
of things over time,

00:59:04.270 --> 00:59:06.950
you would understand little bit better

00:59:06.950 --> 00:59:09.123
about the boundaries there.

00:59:10.840 --> 00:59:11.710
In terms of

00:59:12.650 --> 00:59:16.618
the kind of research environment

00:59:16.618 --> 00:59:19.400
regarding to the IRB,

00:59:19.400 --> 00:59:22.670
we are trying to innovate
IRB a little bit.

00:59:22.670 --> 00:59:25.180
We try to keep the IRB as rigorous,

00:59:25.180 --> 00:59:27.640
just like in a US institutions,

00:59:27.640 --> 00:59:32.380
just because we have to protect
human subjects in general,

00:59:32.380 --> 00:59:33.840
especially in China,

00:59:33.840 --> 00:59:35.513
but we also wanna innovate

00:59:35.513 --> 00:59:38.620
in case you do something with regard to,

00:59:38.620 --> 00:59:39.930
for example, social media

00:59:39.930 --> 00:59:41.490
cause,

00:59:41.490 --> 00:59:42.500
you know,

00:59:42.500 --> 00:59:46.320
the American system did not
probably heard enough about

00:59:46.320 --> 00:59:48.830
IRB regarding to social media as well.

00:59:48.830 --> 00:59:53.490
So if you do something
related to social media

00:59:53.490 --> 00:59:56.000
and the computation on DKU,

00:59:56.000 --> 01:00:00.950
you will get probably
a speedier IRP process

01:00:00.950 --> 01:00:04.150
compared to that same
process in the United States,

01:00:04.150 --> 01:00:08.013
just because we had some new
innovations regarding that.

01:00:11.040 --> 01:00:12.970
- Well we have gone over time

01:00:12.970 --> 01:00:16.010
so I think I'm going to wrap this up.

01:00:16.010 --> 01:00:17.030
But once again,

01:00:17.030 --> 01:00:19.890
I would like to thank all
of our wonderful panelists

01:00:19.890 --> 01:00:23.922
for their super informative
and very interesting talks.

01:00:23.922 --> 01:00:26.450
We'd like to thank the
Council on East Asian Studies,

01:00:26.450 --> 01:00:28.780
especially Stephanie Kim and Richard Sosa

01:00:28.780 --> 01:00:31.670
for providing all the staffing
and support for this event.

01:00:31.670 --> 01:00:33.630
And of course, we would
like to thank all of you

01:00:33.630 --> 01:00:34.810
for tuning in.

01:00:34.810 --> 01:00:37.160
So thank you to all of our supporters

01:00:37.160 --> 01:00:40.915
for making this summer
webinar series a huge success.

01:00:40.915 --> 01:00:44.040
All the best for the rest of your summers.

01:00:44.040 --> 01:00:47.230
And hopefully we will
see you again next year.

01:00:47.230 --> 01:00:48.663
So take care, everyone.

